    "source": "KFF, 'Medicaid and CHIP Income Eligibility Limits for Children as a Percent of the Federal Poverty Level', as of January 2026 (national survey by KFF and the Georgetown University Center for Children and Families)",
    "source_url": "https://www.kff.org/affordable-care-act/state-indicator/medicaid-and-chip-income-eligibility-limits-for-children-as-a-percent-of-the-federal-poverty-level/",
    "source_type": "secondary compilation (survey of state Medicaid/CHIP officials); the CMS Medicaid.gov eligibility-levels table returned 403 from the build sandbox and has not been cross-checked",
    "cfr_reference": "42 CFR 435.118 (children's Medicaid); 42 CFR 457.310, 457.320 (CHIP)",
    "verification_status": "verified",
    "verification_note": "Retrieved 2026-11-17 by the documents pass on issue #18. Internally consistent for all 51 jurisdictions: each row's upper limit equals the highest percentage in that row, and every CHIP-funded Medicaid range starts at or below the Medicaid-funded limit. Limits are MAGI-converted and already include the standard 5-percentage-point disregard at the highest income level, so they are compared directly with household MAGI as a percent of the 2026 poverty guidelines.",
    "how_to_read": [
      "medicaid_funded_pct_fpl: upper limit of regular Medicaid for the age band; any child at or below it is Medicaid-eligible, insured or not.",
      "chip_funded_medicaid_pct_fpl: the income range covered through Medicaid but paid with CHIP funds (Medicaid-expansion CHIP). Only uninsured children qualify above the Medicaid-funded limit.",
      "separate_chip_pct_fpl: upper limit of the state's separate CHIP program (uninsured children); null where the state runs only Medicaid-expansion CHIP."
    ],
    "not_modeled": [
      "CHIP waiting periods and state-employee exclusions",
      "premiums and enrollment fees",
      "immigrant-children options (ICHIA) and the unborn-child option",
      "county-level variation (e.g. higher separate-CHIP limits in some California counties, KFF footnote)",
      "pregnant-women coverage"
    ]
  },
